__learnContent"><header><h1>Minimum Coin Combination</h1></header><div clas=
s=3D"style-module-scss-module__ubvNPq__problemDescription"><p>You are given=
 an array of coin values and a target amount of money. Return the <strong>m=
inimum number of coins</strong> needed to total the target amount. If this =
isn't possible, return =E2=80=901. You may assume there's an unlimited supp=
ly of each coin.</p><h4 id=3D"example-1">Example 1:</h4><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs language-python">Input: coins =3D [<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</spa=
n>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</sp=
an>], target =3D <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>
Output: <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>
</code></pre><p>Explanation: Use one 2-dollar coin and one 3-dollar coin to=
 make 5 dollars.</p><h4 id=3D"example-2">Example 2:</h4><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs language-python">Input: coins =3D [<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</spa=
n>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>], target =3D <span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">5</span>
Output: -<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition---top-down">Intuition - Top-Down</h2>
<p>In this problem, there=E2=80=99s no restriction on the number of coins w=
e can use, which makes a brute force approach that tries every possible coi=
n combination impossible, due to the infinite number of possible combinatio=
ns. This indicates the need for a more efficient method.</p>
<p>Consider the example below:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a simple input for a coding problem, likely rel=
ated to searching or summing within an array.  The input consists of two pa=
rts presented linearly. The first part is an integer array represented by `=
[1 2 3]`, enclosed in square brackets, with the integers 1, 2, and 3 separa=
ted by spaces. This array is followed by a comma and a space, separating it=
 from the second part. The second part specifies a target value, denoted by=
 `target =3D 5`, indicating that the algorithm should search for, or use, t=
he value 5 in relation to the array. There are no URLs or parameters presen=
t; the image solely displays the input data.  The arrangement suggests that=
 the array `[1 2 3]` and the target value `5` are independent inputs to a f=
unction or algorithm." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"192" height=3D"16" decodin=
g=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://byte=
bytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/dynamic-programming/minimum-coin-=
combination/image-15-02-1-JTPEOY5V.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV=
5c"></div></figure>
<p>If we use a 3-dollar coin from the array, then we=E2=80=99ll only need 2=
 dollars more to make 5. This gives us a new target: find the fewest number=
 of coins needed to make 2 dollars:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a simple data flow diagram illustrating a chang=
e in a parameter.  The diagram begins with the word 'use' positioned above =
a downward-pointing arrow, indicating the input or application of a paramet=
er.  The arrow points to the input data: an array `[1 2 3]` and a parameter=
 labeled `target =3D 5`. A solid arrow then connects this input to a new st=
ate, where the array remains the same, `[1 2 3]`, but the `target` paramete=
r has changed to `target =3D 2`.  The diagram visually depicts a process wh=
ere the array remains constant, while the `target` parameter is modified fr=
om 5 to 2, suggesting a change in the input or a transformation within a sy=
stem." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"458" height=3D"45" decoding=3D"async" data=
-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/image=
s/courses/coding-patterns/dynamic-programming/minimum-coin-combination/imag=
e-15-02-2-LUSBRYBO.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></figu=
re>
<p>This indicates we=E2=80=99ve identified subproblems within the main prob=
lem, where <strong>each subproblem requires finding the fewest number of co=
ins needed to make a smaller target</strong>.</p>
<p>Each coin we use creates a new subproblem. For example, using a 1-dollar=
 coin changes our target from 5 to 4 dollars. Let's visualize how these sma=
ller targets, representing new subproblems, are created after using each co=
in:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a tree-like diagram illustrating a decompositio=
n process.  At the top is a central node labeled 'target =3D 5,' representi=
ng an initial target value.  Three downward-pointing lines, labeled 'use 1,=
' 'use 2,' and 'use 3' in orange text, connect this central node to three l=
eaf nodes at the bottom. Each leaf node represents a sub-target value resul=
ting from the decomposition: 'target =3D 4' on the left, 'target =3D 3' in =
the center (directly below the central node), and 'target =3D 2' on the rig=
